  {
   "url": "https://wdfw.wa.gov/places-to-go/wildlife-areas/leque-island-wildlife-area-unit",
   "status": 200,
   "final_url": "https://wdfw.wa.gov/places-to-go/wildlife-areas/leque-island-wildlife-area-unit",
   "title": "Leque Island Wildlife Area Unit | Washington Department of Fish & Wildlife",
   "text": "Skip to main content\nSite search\nMain navigation\nHome\nSpecies & Habitats\nFishing & Shellfishing\nHunting\nLicenses & Permits\nPlaces to go\nTop menu\nAbout WDFW\nNewsroom\nGet involved\nCivil Rights/Accessibility\nPlaces to go\nWildlife areas\nLeque Island Wildlife Area Unit\nLeque Island Wildlife Area Unit\nAlerts\nLeque Island restoration project updates\nVisitors should exercise extreme caution as conditions on the unit have changed following habitat restoration work. Read full alert\nImage\nImage\nPhoto by WDFW\nCaption\nView of Leque Island from the air\nThe Leque Island Unit consists of wetlands and tidal marsh habitat. This is a popular site for waterfowl hunting, wildlife observation and photography, and walking. The habitat provides areas for wildlife ranging from waterfowl, mammals, raptors, and passerine birds.\nEstuary habitat restoration work has been undertaken through the Leque Island Restoration Project, which restored much of the unit south of Highway 532 in 2019 as well as the area north of the highway in 2022.\nAdditional estuary restoration projects are now being planned by WDFW and the Stillaguamish Tribe of Indians to further improve salmon habitat and fish passage.\nThe unit is located west of Stanwood, between Port Susan and Skagit bays, in Snohomish County. The unit can be accessed from Highway 532 between Stanwood and Camano Island.\nThis unit is part of the Skagit Wildlife Area.\nGame Management Unit: 407\nAcres: 471\nEsri, NASA, NGA, USGS, FEMA | Island County, WA State Parks GIS, Esri, TomTom, Garmin, SafeGraph, GeoTechnologies, Inc, METI/NASA, USGS, Bureau of Land Management, EPA, NPS, US Census Bureau, USDA, USFWS | Washington Department of Fish and Wildlife\nPowered by Esri\nGetting there\nMaps and directions to WDFW-managed access points for this unit. Opens Google Maps in a new tab.\nAccess\nContact\nGreg Meis\nWildlife Area Manager\n425-775-1311\nMt. Vernon, WA 98273\nRecreation and public access\nConservation\nLand stewardship\nPlaces to go\nLife Outdoors Report\nWDFW event calendar\nAlerts\nWildlife areas\nWater access areas\nClam, mussel, and oyster harvest\nPublic fishing piers\nSalmon viewing locations\nLowland lakes\nHigh lakes\nMarine areas\nLicense dealers\nTop tasks\nLog in to the WILD licensing website\nGet razor clam information\nFind a wildlife area\nEvent calendar\nSubmit a photo\nReport a website error\nAbout WDFW\nContact us\nRegional offices\nEnforcement\nMedia resources\nFish and Wildlife Commission\nPublic Records Requests\nJobs at WDFW\nRule making\nWebsite accessibility\nRecursos en Español\nRequest translations or interpretation\nStay connected\nWDFW\non Facebook\n@TheWDFW\non Instagram\nWDFW Police\non Facebook\n@WDFW\non Twitter\nTheWDFW\non YouTube\nWDFW Blog\non Medium\nJoin our email lists\nWDFW on\nLinkedIn\n© 2026 All rights reserved.",
